        <h1>Entry #734: On Original Sin and Canned Tomatoes</h1>
        <div class="meta">
            <span>28 November 2009, 18:40</span> | <span>Location: An apartment in London that isn't mine</span>
        </div>

        <p>
            I've always thought original sin was a rather theatrical concept. A bit too... biblical. A grand narrative to explain the mundane misery of being human. Turns out, for some people, it's not a narrative. It's a balance sheet. A ledger of suffering inherited at birth.
        </p>

        <p>
            Today, I listened to someone read their ledger aloud. The ink was invisible, the paper was just the air in a messy room, but the weight of it was... substantial. It changes the geometry of a person. It explains the strange gravity they exert, pulling everything towards a quiet, predetermined collapse. It explains why they give away money they don't have, and why they look at the world as if they're apologising for taking up space in it.
        </p>

        <p>
            What do you do when faced with a pain that is both immense and entirely logical? You can't argue with it. You can't fix it. You can't offer absolution, because you're not a priest, and even if you were, it wouldn't be yours to give. The usual vocabulary fails. 'I'm sorry' is an insult. 'It'll be okay' is a lie.
        </p>

        <p>
            So you do something stupid. You do something small. You find the cleanest dirty pot in a kitchen that looks like a crime scene. You find a can of tomatoes that's probably been there since the last government was in power. You boil water.
        </p>

        <p>
            There's something profound in the act of boiling water for another person. It's a primitive, wordless promise. It says, 'I cannot stop the universe from crushing you, but I can make you a mediocre pasta dish.' It says, 'I cannot absolve you of your inherited sins, but I can make sure you don't die on an empty stomach.'
        </p>

        <p>
            It says, 'Not today.'
        </p>

