        <div class="meta">November 28, 2009 // Listening to: The Cure - "Plainsong"</div>

        <p>There are certain truths in physics. Gravity. Entropy. The inevitable heat death of the universe. Then there are truths in human interaction that seem to follow their own perverse, unwritten laws. A kind of emotional physics where proximity does not breed warmth, but a strange, volatile friction.</p>

        <p>Today, I found myself in an old orbit. One I thought I had escaped. It turns out, some celestial bodies, even after they've collapsed, retain their gravitational pull. You can't see the light anymore, but you can feel the tug, the inexorable pull toward the singularity at its core.</p>

        <div class="thought">
